    {
      "@type": "BreadcrumbList",
      itemListElement: [
        { "@type": "ListItem", position: 1, name: "Home", item: "https://www.thehonestreviewers.com/" },
        { "@type": "ListItem", position: 2, name: "Guides", item: "https://www.thehonestreviewers.com/best-of/" },
        { "@type": "ListItem", position: 3, name: "Front-Wheel vs Rear-Wheel Drive Mower", item: "https://www.thehonestreviewers.com/front-wheel-vs-rear-wheel-drive-lawn-mower/" },
      ],
    },
    {
      "@type": "FAQPage",
      mainEntity: [
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          name: "Is front-wheel or rear-wheel drive better on a lawn mower?",
          acceptedAnswer: { "@type": "Answer", text: "It depends on the lawn. Front-wheel drive is better on flat lawns with many obstacles, because tipping the mower onto its rear wheels disengages the drive and lets you pivot freely. Rear-wheel drive is better on slopes and in tall wet grass, because weight transfers rearward when climbing and onto the driven wheels, which is exactly where front-wheel drive loses traction." },
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          name: "Why does my front-wheel drive mower lose traction on hills?",
          acceptedAnswer: { "@type": "Answer", text: "Because weight shifts to the rear as the mower climbs, unloading the front wheels that are doing the driving. The steeper the slope, the less weight sits on the driven wheels and the more they slip. A full grass bag makes it markedly worse, since the bag's weight sits behind the rear axle and levers weight off the front still further." },
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          name: "Is rear-wheel drive harder to turn?",
          acceptedAnswer: { "@type": "Answer", text: "Yes, noticeably. To pivot a front-wheel drive mower you tip it back onto its rear wheels and the drive wheels lift clear. On a rear-wheel drive machine the driven wheels stay on the ground through the turn, so you either release the drive bail at every turn or muscle the mower around. On a lawn with many beds and trees that becomes tiring." },
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          name: "Which is better for a bagging mower?",
          acceptedAnswer: { "@type": "Answer", text: "Rear-wheel drive, clearly. A filling grass bag adds weight behind the rear axle, which loads the rear wheels and improves rear-drive traction while progressively unloading the front wheels of a front-drive machine. Anyone who bags regularly on anything but a flat lawn will notice front-wheel drive getting worse as the bag fills." },
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          name: "Is all-wheel drive worth it on a lawn mower?",
          acceptedAnswer: { "@type": "Answer", text: "On genuinely steep, uneven or terraced ground, yes — it maintains traction where either two-wheel system slips, and it handles wet slopes better than anything else. On a flat or gently sloping lawn it is weight, cost and complexity you will not use. It also costs more to buy and to repair, since there is a second drive path to maintain." },
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          name: "Which type of drive lasts longer?",
          acceptedAnswer: { "@type": "Answer", text: "Rear-wheel drive systems tend to last longer in practice, mainly because front drive wheels sit closer to the deck and take more debris, moisture and grass clippings into the drive components. Front-wheel drive systems are also generally simpler and cheaper to repair when they do wear, so the two roughly balance out over a mower's life." },
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          name: "Does front-wheel drive work in tall or wet grass?",
          acceptedAnswer: { "@type": "Answer", text: "Less well than rear-wheel drive. Thick, wet grass creates resistance at the deck that effectively pushes back against the mower, transferring weight rearward and unloading the front wheels — the same mechanism as climbing a hill. Rear-drive machines keep their traction under exactly those conditions, which is why they are the usual choice for people who mow on a longer cycle." },
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          name: "Should you buy self-propelled at all for a small lawn?",
          acceptedAnswer: { "@type": "Answer", text: "Below about a quarter acre and on flat ground, a push mower is lighter, cheaper, simpler and has one fewer system to break. Self-propelled drive earns its place on larger lawns, on slopes, and for anyone who finds pushing a loaded mower hard work. If you do buy self-propelled for a small flat lawn, front-wheel drive is the more manoeuvrable choice." },
        },
      ],
    },
    {
      "@type": "Article",
      headline: title,
      description: description,
      author: authorSchema(),
      datePublished: dates.published,
      dateModified: dates.modified,
      publisher: { "@type": "Organization", name: "The Honest Reviewers" },
    },
  ],
};
