        <div class="mt-16 bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-8">
          <h2 class="text-2xl font-black text-white mb-4">The Bottom Line</h2>
          <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed mb-4">
            <strong class="text-white">Slope decides it.</strong> Climbing transfers weight onto rear drive wheels and away from front ones, so rear-wheel drive gains traction exactly where front-wheel drive loses it. Any lawn with a real gradient wants rear-wheel drive.
          </p>
          <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed mb-4">
            <strong class="text-white">Obstacles are the counterweight.</strong> Front-wheel drive lets you tip the mower back and pivot without releasing anything, which on a lawn full of beds and trees happens dozens of times a cut. On a flat obstacle course, that convenience is worth more than traction you never need.
          </p>
          <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed mb-4">
            If you bag every cut, add that to the rear-wheel column regardless of slope. If your lawn genuinely splits the difference, choose rear-wheel drive — you adapt to the turning in a week, but you never adapt to a mower that will not climb your garden.
          </p>
